# The ledger of `protobuf`: what Go's protobuf module (google.golang.org/protobuf
# 1.36.12: proto, encoding/protowire, reflect/protodesc, reflect/protoregistry,
# types/dynamicpb, encoding/protojson, encoding/prototext, types/known/*), Rust's
# prost 0.14 with prost-types and the protobuf crate 3, and Python's
# google.protobuf 7.36.2 (message, descriptor, descriptor_pool, json_format,
# text_format, message_factory, proto, proto_json, the well-known types' modules)
# let a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. None of
# them is in its language's standard library: each was fetched into the scratch
# folder on 2026-11-16, Go's with `go get`, Rust's documentation with `cargo
# doc`, Python's with `pip install --target`, and listed with `go doc`, the
# documentation's item files and `dir()`. No `.proto` reader is in any of the
# three (protoc reads them), so `bufbuild/protocompile` stood in for protoc as
# the oracle of reading one. Every expected value of the tests was observed from
# Go's runtime or, where Go and Python differ, from Python's upb runtime, on
# 2026-11-17.

capability wire/varint  a varint written, read, and measured
  heroes  protobuf.push_varint, protobuf.decode_varint, protobuf.size_of_varint, protobuf.WireNumber
  go      protowire.AppendVarint, protowire.ConsumeVarint, protowire.SizeVarint
  rust    protobuf::CodedOutputStream::write_raw_varint64, protobuf::CodedInputStream::read_raw_varint64, protobuf::rt::compute_raw_varint64_size
  python  -
  note    a varint written longer than it needs is read, up to ten bytes; an eleventh byte, or a tenth above 1, fails `invalid`, where Go's ConsumeVarint answers a negative length (observed: `ffffffffffffffffff02` and `8080808080808080808000` refused)

capability wire/zigzag  the zig-zag form of `sint32` and `sint64`
  heroes  protobuf.to_zigzag, protobuf.from_zigzag
  go      protowire.EncodeZigZag, protowire.DecodeZigZag
  rust    protobuf::CodedInputStream::read_sint64, protobuf::CodedOutputStream::write_sint64_no_tag
  python  -

capability wire/fixed  four and eight bytes, the lowest first
  heroes  protobuf.push_fixed32, protobuf.push_fixed64, protobuf.decode_fixed32, protobuf.decode_fixed64
  go      protowire.AppendFixed32, protowire.AppendFixed64, protowire.ConsumeFixed32, protowire.ConsumeFixed64, protowire.SizeFixed32, protowire.SizeFixed64
  rust    protobuf::CodedInputStream::read_fixed32, protobuf::CodedInputStream::read_fixed64, protobuf::CodedOutputStream::write_fixed32_no_tag, protobuf::CodedOutputStream::write_fixed64_no_tag
  python  -

capability wire/float-bits  a float's and a double's IEEE 754 bits, as a fixed field holds them
  heroes  numbers.f32_to_bits, numbers.bits_to_f32, numbers.f64_to_bits, numbers.bits_to_f64
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `numbers`' since 2026-11-18, where this package's own four, `bits_of_float`, `float_of_bits`, `bits_of_double` and `double_of_bits`, moved so that `sql/mysql` and every other reader of a binary format share one (README A5); the same answers: exact arithmetic on powers of two, every `nan` the quiet one, `0x7fc00000` and `0x7ff8000000000000`, its sign and payload not kept, since the language reads neither

capability wire/tag  a field's tag written and read: its number and its wire type
  heroes  protobuf.push_tag, protobuf.decode_tag, protobuf.WireTag, protobuf.WireType, protobuf.MAX_FIELD_NUMBER
  go      protowire.AppendTag, protowire.ConsumeTag, protowire.EncodeTag, protowire.DecodeTag, protowire.SizeTag, protowire.Number, protowire.Number.IsValid, protowire.Type, protowire.VarintType, protowire.Fixed32Type, protowire.Fixed64Type, protowire.BytesType, protowire.StartGroupType, protowire.EndGroupType, protowire.MinValidNumber, protowire.MaxValidNumber, protowire.FirstReservedNumber, protowire.LastReservedNumber, protowire.DefaultRecursionLimit
  rust    protobuf::rt::WireType, protobuf::rt::tag_size, protobuf::CodedInputStream::read_raw_tag_or_eof
  python  -
  note    a number past 2^29 - 1 fails `invalid`, which Go's ConsumeTag answers and its Unmarshal refuses (observed: `80808080108a01` refused by both)

capability wire/length-delimited  bytes after their length
  heroes  protobuf.push_length_delimited, protobuf.decode_length_delimited, protobuf.WireBytes
  go      protowire.AppendBytes, protowire.AppendString, protowire.ConsumeBytes, protowire.ConsumeString, protowire.SizeBytes
  rust    protobuf::rt::bytes_size, protobuf::rt::string_size, protobuf::CodedInputStream::read_bytes, protobuf::CodedInputStream::read_string
  python  -
  note    a length past the bytes left fails before anything is held for it

capability wire/skip  a field passed over, a group to its own end tag
  heroes  protobuf.skip_field
  go      protowire.ConsumeField, protowire.ConsumeFieldValue, protowire.ConsumeGroup, protowire.AppendGroup, protowire.SizeGroup, protowire.ParseError
  rust    protobuf::rt::skip_field_for_tag, protobuf::rt::read_unknown_or_skip_group
  python  -
  note    groups nested deeper than `DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH` fail `invalid`; Go's limit is 10,000, C++'s and Python's 100

capability wire/raw  a message read and written with no schema, as `protoc --decode_raw` reads it
  heroes  protobuf.decode_raw, protobuf.encode_raw, protobuf.RawField, protobuf.RawValue
  go      -
  rust    protobuf::UnknownFields, protobuf::UnknownValue, protobuf::UnknownValueRef
  python  unknown_fields.UnknownFieldSet
  note    a group is a value holding its fields, read on a list the package holds; a length-delimited value is not looked inside, since the wire does not say whether it is a string, bytes, a message or a packed list

capability wire/delimited-stream  messages one after another, each after its length
  heroes  protobuf.encode_delimited, protobuf.decode_delimited, protobuf.Decoded
  go      -
  rust    prost::encode_length_delimiter, prost::decode_length_delimiter, prost::length_delimiter_len, prost::Message::encode_length_delimited, prost::Message::decode_length_delimited, protobuf::Message::write_length_delimited_to_vec, protobuf::Message::parse_from_bytes
  python  proto.serialize_length_prefixed, proto.parse_length_prefixed
  note    Go keeps this in encoding/protodelim, outside the packages censused; the bytes are held whole, and `io` reads them from a stream

capability schema/read-proto  `.proto` files read at run time into a schema, imports through a function the program gives
  heroes  protobuf.parse_schema, protobuf.parse_schema_text, protobuf.parse_schema_texts, protobuf.Schema
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    protoc's work, which none of the three libraries holds: proto2, proto3, editions 2023 and 2024, packages, imports public and weak, nested types, enums with aliases, oneofs, map fields, proto3 `optional`, groups, extensions and extension ranges, reserved numbers and names, options, services with streams; names resolved by protoc's scoping rules and seen only through imports; cycles, duplicates, reserved and out-of-range numbers refused. The files read were compiled by protocompile alongside, and every encoding compared

capability schema/editions  an edition's features honoured where they change the wire or JSON
  heroes  protobuf.Field, protobuf.EnumType
  go      protodesc.NewFile (editions), protodesc.FileOptions
  rust    -
  python  descriptor_pool.DescriptorPool.SetFeatureSetDefaults, descriptor.FieldDescriptor.has_presence, descriptor.FieldDescriptor.is_packed
  note    field_presence (EXPLICIT, IMPLICIT, LEGACY_REQUIRED), repeated_field_encoding, utf8_validation, message_encoding (DELIMITED written as a group), enum_type and json_format, at file, message, oneof, enum and field; proto2 and proto3 read as the editions they stand for. Features no wire reads (enforce_naming_style, default_symbol_visibility) are kept as options and not checked

capability schema/carried-files  the well-known types' files imported without being given
  heroes  protobuf.parse_schema
  go      types/known/anypb.File_google_protobuf_any_proto, durationpb.File_google_protobuf_duration_proto, timestamppb.File_google_protobuf_timestamp_proto, structpb.File_google_protobuf_struct_proto, wrapperspb.File_google_protobuf_wrappers_proto, fieldmaskpb.File_google_protobuf_field_mask_proto, emptypb.File_google_protobuf_empty_proto
  rust    prost_types::Any, prost_types::Duration, prost_types::Timestamp, prost_types::Struct, prost_types::Value, prost_types::ListValue, prost_types::FieldMask
  python  any_pb2, duration_pb2, timestamp_pb2, struct_pb2, wrappers_pb2, field_mask_pb2, empty_pb2
  note    any, duration, empty, field_mask, struct, timestamp and wrappers carried, written from their documented fields; descriptor.proto carried only as its option messages, which a custom option extends

capability schema/carried-api-type  the well-known files api.proto, type.proto and source_context.proto carried
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    prost_types::Api, prost_types::Method, prost_types::Mixin, prost_types::Type, prost_types::Field, prost_types::Enum, prost_types::EnumValue, prost_types::Option, prost_types::SourceContext, prost_types::Syntax, prost_types::field::Kind, prost_types::field::Cardinality
  python  -
  note    a program gives their text through `open` meanwhile

capability schema/lookup  a message, an enum, a service, a file or an extension found by name
  heroes  protobuf.message_named, protobuf.enum_named, protobuf.service_named, protobuf.file_named, protobuf.extension_named
  go      protoregistry.Files.FindDescriptorByName, protoregistry.Files.FindFileByPath, protoregistry.Types.FindMessageByName, protoregistry.Types.FindEnumByName, protoregistry.Types.FindExtensionByName, protoregistry.Types.FindMessageByURL, dynamicpb.Types.FindMessageByName, dynamicpb.Types.FindEnumByName, dynamicpb.Types.FindExtensionByName, dynamicpb.Types.FindMessageByURL, protoregistry.NotFound
  rust    protobuf::reflect::FileDescriptor::message_by_package_relative_name, protobuf::reflect::FileDescriptor::enum_by_package_relative_name
  python  descriptor_pool.DescriptorPool.FindMessageTypeByName, FindEnumTypeByName, FindServiceByName, FindFileByName, FindExtensionByName, FindFileContainingSymbol
  note    absent is `not_found`; a leading dot is taken off. A URL is resolved by its last part, as `unpack_any` does

capability schema/members  a field, a method or an enum value found in its declaration
  heroes  protobuf.field_named, protobuf.field_numbered, protobuf.field_of, protobuf.method_named, protobuf.enum_value_named, protobuf.enum_value_numbered
  go      protoreflect.FieldDescriptors.ByName, protoreflect.FieldDescriptors.ByNumber, protoreflect.FieldDescriptors.ByJSONName, protoreflect.MethodDescriptors.ByName, protoreflect.EnumValueDescriptors.ByName, protoreflect.EnumValueDescriptors.ByNumber, protoregistry.Types.FindExtensionByNumber
  rust    protobuf::reflect::MessageDescriptor::field_by_name, protobuf::reflect::MessageDescriptor::field_by_number, protobuf::reflect::EnumDescriptor::value_by_name, protobuf::reflect::EnumDescriptor::value_by_number
  python  descriptor_pool.DescriptorPool.FindFieldByName, FindMethodByName, FindOneofByName, FindExtensionByNumber, descriptor.Descriptor.fields_by_name, descriptor.EnumDescriptor.values_by_name
  note    an extension is found by its full name where `field_of` meets a dot

capability schema/descriptors  the declarations as values a program reads
  heroes  protobuf.File, protobuf.MessageType, protobuf.Field, protobuf.Oneof, protobuf.EnumType, protobuf.EnumValue, protobuf.Service, protobuf.Method, protobuf.Kind, protobuf.Cardinality, protobuf.Syntax, protobuf.Range, protobuf.OptionSetting, protobuf.Constant
  go      protoreflect.FileDescriptor, MessageDescriptor, FieldDescriptor, OneofDescriptor, EnumDescriptor, EnumValueDescriptor, ServiceDescriptor, MethodDescriptor, ExtensionDescriptor, Kind, Cardinality, Syntax, FullName, Name, FieldNumber
  rust    protobuf::reflect::FileDescriptor, MessageDescriptor, FieldDescriptor, OneofDescriptor, EnumDescriptor, EnumValueDescriptor, ServiceDescriptor, MethodDescriptor, RuntimeFieldType, RuntimeType, Syntax
  python  descriptor.FileDescriptor, Descriptor, FieldDescriptor, OneofDescriptor, EnumDescriptor, EnumValueDescriptor, ServiceDescriptor, MethodDescriptor
  note    a field's presence, packing, UTF-8 check, JSON name and default are decided once, so a program reads them and never the features; a record of the package holds no handle back to its parent, so a field names its oneof and a type its file by name

capability schema/json-name  a field's name in JSON
  heroes  protobuf.Field
  go      protoreflect.FieldDescriptor.JSONName
  rust    protobuf::reflect::FieldDescriptor::json_name
  python  descriptor.FieldDescriptor.json_name, descriptor.FieldDescriptor.camelcase_name
  note    protoc's rule: each `_` taken out and the letter after it a capital; `json_name` read where written; two fields of one JSON name refused unless json_format is LEGACY_BEST_EFFORT

capability schema/custom-options  a custom option's value read as its extension's type
  heroes  pending
  go      proto.GetExtension (on options)
  rust    protobuf::ext::ExtFieldOptional, protobuf::ext::ExtFieldRepeated
  python  descriptor.FieldDescriptor.GetOptions
  note    every option is kept as written, `OptionSetting` and its `Constant`, an aggregate's text included; reading the text format into the extension's type waits for the text format

capability schema/descriptor-set-read  a `FileDescriptorSet` that protoc wrote read into a schema
  heroes  pending
  go      protodesc.NewFile, protodesc.NewFiles, protodesc.FileOptions.New, protodesc.FileOptions.NewFiles, protodesc.Resolver
  rust    prost_types::FileDescriptorSet, prost_types::FileDescriptorProto, protobuf::reflect::FileDescriptor::new_dynamic
  python  descriptor_pool.DescriptorPool.Add, descriptor_pool.DescriptorPool.AddSerializedFile, descriptor.MakeDescriptor
  note    it needs descriptor.proto whole, of which the package carries the option messages alone

capability schema/descriptor-set-write  a schema written as `FileDescriptorProto`s, as gRPC's reflection serves them
  heroes  pending
  go      protodesc.ToFileDescriptorProto, ToDescriptorProto, ToEnumDescriptorProto, ToEnumValueDescriptorProto, ToFieldDescriptorProto, ToMethodDescriptorProto, ToOneofDescriptorProto, ToServiceDescriptorProto
  rust    prost_types::DescriptorProto, EnumDescriptorProto, FieldDescriptorProto, OneofDescriptorProto, ServiceDescriptorProto, MethodDescriptorProto, FileOptions, MessageOptions, FieldOptions, OneofOptions, EnumOptions, EnumValueOptions, ServiceOptions, MethodOptions, ExtensionRangeOptions, UninterpretedOption, SourceCodeInfo, GeneratedCodeInfo
  python  descriptor.FileDescriptor.serialized_pb, descriptor.Descriptor.CopyToProto
  note    the same descriptor.proto is owed first

capability message/new  an empty message of a type the schema holds
  heroes  protobuf.new_message, protobuf.Message
  go      dynamicpb.NewMessage, dynamicpb.NewMessageType, dynamicpb.NewTypes, dynamicpb.Message.New, dynamicpb.Message.Type, proto.MessageName
  rust    protobuf::reflect::MessageDescriptor::new_instance, protobuf::MessageDyn
  python  message_factory.GetMessageClass, message_factory.GetMessageClassesForFiles, message_factory.GetMessages, message.Message
  note    a message is a tree of values, as `json.Value` is, compared by `==`; its type is named, and the schema is given beside it to every function that reads its type

capability message/fields  a field read, set, tested and cleared by name
  heroes  protobuf.get, protobuf.set, protobuf.has, protobuf.clear
  go      dynamicpb.Message.Get, dynamicpb.Message.Set, dynamicpb.Message.Has, dynamicpb.Message.Clear, dynamicpb.Message.Mutable, dynamicpb.Message.NewField, dynamicpb.Message.Range
  rust    protobuf::reflect::FieldDescriptor::get_singular_field_or_default, protobuf::reflect::FieldDescriptor::set_singular_field, protobuf::reflect::FieldDescriptor::has_field, protobuf::reflect::FieldDescriptor::clear_field, protobuf::reflect::ReflectFieldRef
  python  message.Message.HasField, message.Message.ClearField, message.Message.ListFields, proto.clear_field
  note    `set` checks the value's kind against the field's and puts it in the protocol's form: a field without presence at its default is cleared, a oneof's others cleared, a map's entries ordered by key; a closed enum's number it does not name is refused. Go's Range is `keys(m.fields)`

capability message/typed-setters  an integer, a float or an enum's name set at the field's own width
  heroes  protobuf.set_int, protobuf.set_uint, protobuf.set_float, protobuf.set_enum, protobuf.enum_name_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    what makes moving a record into a message short: the program writes `set_int(schema, @m, field: Person::id, n: p.id)` whatever the `.proto`'s integer kind, and a number past it fails `does_not_fit`

capability message/records  a record of the program moved into a message and out of one by its checked field names
  heroes  protobuf.set_int
  go      -
  rust    prost::Message (derived), protobuf::Message (generated)
  python  -
  note    the language has no reflection and Rebel no code generator, a second tool beside `heroes`; a program writes its two functions with `Record::field` names (spec § 9), which the compiler checks, as the example of `set_int` shows

capability message/oneof  which field of a oneof a message holds
  heroes  protobuf.which_oneof
  go      dynamicpb.Message.WhichOneof
  rust    protobuf::reflect::OneofDescriptor
  python  message.Message.WhichOneof
  note    none held is `not_found`

capability message/lists-maps  a repeated field added to, a map's entries put and read
  heroes  protobuf.add_item, protobuf.put_entry, protobuf.entry_value, protobuf.items_of, protobuf.entries_of, protobuf.Entry
  go      protoreflect.List, protoreflect.Map, protoreflect.MapKey
  rust    protobuf::reflect::ReflectRepeatedRef, ReflectRepeatedMut, ReflectMapRef, ReflectMapMut
  python  RepeatedCompositeFieldContainer.add, append, extend, map containers
  note    `add_item` and `put_entry` cost the field's length, since a value is a copy; a long list is built as a `[Value]` and `set` once

capability message/values  a field's value read as the language's own types
  heroes  protobuf.Value, protobuf.kind_of, protobuf.bool_of, protobuf.int_of, protobuf.uint_of, protobuf.float_of, protobuf.text_of, protobuf.bytes_of, protobuf.message_of
  go      protoreflect.Value, protoreflect.ValueOf, ValueOfBool, ValueOfInt32, ValueOfInt64, ValueOfUint32, ValueOfUint64, ValueOfFloat32, ValueOfFloat64, ValueOfString, ValueOfBytes, ValueOfEnum, ValueOfMessage, ValueOfList, ValueOfMap, Value.Bool, Value.Int, Value.Uint, Value.Float, Value.String, Value.Bytes, Value.Enum, Value.Message, Value.List, Value.Map, Value.Interface, Value.IsValid
  rust    protobuf::reflect::ReflectValueBox, protobuf::reflect::ReflectValueRef, protobuf::reflect::ProtobufValue
  python  -
  note    each number keeps its wire width, so a value read and written again is the same bytes; a `string` whose bytes are not UTF-8, where its file does not check them (proto2's), is kept as `.binary`, never lost

capability message/merge  one message merged into another
  heroes  protobuf.merge
  go      proto.Merge, proto.UnmarshalOptions.Merge
  rust    prost::Message::merge, protobuf::Message::merge_from_bytes
  python  message.Message.MergeFrom, message.Message.MergeFromString
  note    defined as the protocol defines it, the two encodings read as one

capability message/equal-copy-reset  two messages compared, a message copied, a message emptied
  heroes  built-in ==
  go      proto.Equal, proto.Clone, proto.CloneOf, proto.Reset, dynamicpb.Message.Reset
  rust    protobuf::reflect::ReflectEq, protobuf::reflect::ReflectEqMode, Clone
  python  message.Message.CopyFrom, message.Message.Clear, proto.clear_message
  note    a message is a value: `=` copies it, `==` compares it (maps whatever their order, a map field's entries kept in key order), and `new_message` is an empty one; two `nan` are not equal, as `==` answers on any float

capability message/required  a message checked for the fields its type requires
  heroes  protobuf.check_required
  go      proto.CheckInitialized, dynamicpb.Message.IsValid
  rust    protobuf::Message::is_initialized, protobuf::Message::check_initialized
  python  message.Message.IsInitialized, message.Message.FindInitializationErrors
  note    the first missing field named by its path, `legacy.Req.id`

capability message/unknown  fields the type does not declare kept, read and dropped
  heroes  protobuf.Message, protobuf.clear_unknown
  go      dynamicpb.Message.GetUnknown, dynamicpb.Message.SetUnknown, proto.UnmarshalOptions.DiscardUnknown, protoreflect.RawFields
  rust    protobuf::UnknownFields, protobuf::Message::special_fields
  python  message.Message.UnknownFields, message.Message.DiscardUnknownFields
  note    kept as the bytes they were read from and written after the known fields; a closed enum's number it does not name goes there, as C++, Java and Python's upb put it (Go's dynamicpb keeps it in the field: observed `{"size":3}` against Python's `{}`)

capability message/extensions  an extension read and set on the message it extends
  heroes  protobuf.get, protobuf.set, protobuf.has, protobuf.clear, protobuf.extension_named
  go      proto.GetExtension, proto.SetExtension, proto.HasExtension, proto.ClearExtension, proto.RangeExtensions, dynamicpb.NewExtensionType
  rust    protobuf::ext::ExtFieldOptional, protobuf::ext::ExtFieldRepeated
  python  message.Message.HasExtension, message.Message.ClearExtension, message.Message.Extensions
  note    named by the extension's full name, `legacy.priority`, and held among the fields by number

capability codec/encode  a message encoded, its fields in the order of their numbers
  heroes  protobuf.encode, protobuf.encode_partial
  go      proto.Marshal, proto.MarshalOptions, proto.MarshalOptions.Marshal, proto.MarshalOptions.MarshalAppend, proto.MarshalOptions.Size, proto.Size
  rust    prost::Message::encode, prost::Message::encode_to_vec, prost::Message::encoded_len, protobuf::Message::write_to_bytes, protobuf::Message::compute_size
  python  message.Message.SerializeToString, message.Message.SerializePartialToString, message.Message.ByteSize, proto.serialize, proto.byte_size
  note    always deterministic, a map's entries by key; fields by number, extensions among them, as C++ and Python write them (Go writes extensions first and oneofs last; observed). A message's size is `encode(...).len()`

capability codec/decode  bytes decoded by a type, as strictly as a program asks
  heroes  protobuf.decode, protobuf.decode_with, protobuf.strict, protobuf.DecodeOptions, protobuf.DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH
  go      proto.Unmarshal, proto.UnmarshalOptions, proto.UnmarshalOptions.Unmarshal
  rust    prost::Message::decode, prost::Message::merge, protobuf::Message::parse_from_bytes, protobuf::Message::merge_from_bytes, protobuf::CodedInputStream
  python  message.Message.ParseFromString, message.Message.FromString, proto.parse
  note    the last of a scalar written twice, a message written twice merged, packed and unpacked lists both read, a field of a wire type its kind does not take kept as unknown (Go the same, observed), a string that must be UTF-8 and is not `not_text`; nesting stops at 100 as C++ and Python stop, Go at 10,000

capability json/write  a message written in the proto3 JSON mapping
  heroes  protobuf.to_json, protobuf.to_json_with, protobuf.json_options, protobuf.JsonOptions
  go      protojson.Marshal, protojson.Format, protojson.MarshalOptions, protojson.MarshalOptions.Marshal, protojson.MarshalOptions.MarshalAppend, protojson.MarshalOptions.Format
  rust    -
  python  json_format.MessageToJson, json_format.MessageToDict, proto_json.serialize
  note    a `json.Value`, which `json.format` and `json.format_indented` write (Go's Multiline and Indent); members in the order of their numbers, 64-bit integers as texts, a `float` as the shortest decimal of its f32, `nan` as "NaN"; `emit_defaults` is Go's EmitDefaultValues and Python's always_print_fields_with_no_presence; byte for byte Go's output in every case compared but Go's random white space

capability json/emit-unpopulated  unset message and oneof fields written as `null`
  heroes  pending
  go      protojson.MarshalOptions.EmitUnpopulated
  rust    -
  python  -

capability json/read  a message read from the proto3 JSON mapping
  heroes  protobuf.from_json, protobuf.from_json_with, protobuf.json_read_options, protobuf.JsonReadOptions
  go      protojson.Unmarshal, protojson.UnmarshalOptions, protojson.UnmarshalOptions.Unmarshal
  rust    -
  python  json_format.Parse, json_format.ParseDict, json_format.ParseError, proto_json.parse
  note    from a `json.Value`, which `json.parse` reads, so a key written twice is refused there; a 64-bit integer written as a number past 2^53 reaches the mapping as an f64, and is read exactly only by parsing with `json.ParseOptions.exact_integers`, which refuses it. A name the enum does not hold is `not_found` (Go's `invalid`); a float past an f32 `does_not_fit`

